; A line is of a retired kind unless its bike is an object.
pub fn order_presentation_line_is_retired(bike_is_an_object: bool) bool {
  return !bike_is_an_object;
}

; How many fields a line of a retired kind is served with: the neutral name, and each kept figure
; the stored line holds. Never more, whatever else it stored.
pub fn order_presentation_retired_line_field_count(quantity_stored: bool, price_stored: bool) u8 {
  if (quantity_stored and price_stored) {
    return 3;
  }
  if (quantity_stored or price_stored) {
    return 2;
  }
  return 1;
}

; --- An order of the previous shop is not shown at all (owner, 2026-09-26) ----------------------
; DECIDED 2026-09-26. Asked about the lines served under the neutral name above, the owner asked
; why they are there at all (verbatim, in Russian, in the rules' module header, RETIRED_LINE_RULE;
; this file stays ASCII). The operator read it as PREVIOUS_SHOP_ORDER_READ_AS: a customer must not
; see the previous shop's orders at all. So the rule moved from the LINE to the ORDER. An order is
; the previous shop's when its stored shop names the previous shop (the test customer_shop_id
; already makes) or when none of its lines is a bike line -- every line of a retired kind, an empty
; list, or items that are not a list. The previous shop rented no bikes, the checkout refuses an
; empty cart, and since migration 085 it can store no other line, so an order this code cannot
; vouch for is not shown: the same fail-closed reading as every rule above. The rule is
; customer_sees_order in RETIRED_LINE_RULE, asked through Order::shown_to_customer
; (PREVIOUS_SHOP_ORDER_SERVER_SITE) by every customer read of an order
; (PREVIOUS_SHOP_ORDER_CUSTOMER_READS):
;   * the list skips such an order a page at a time, newest first, so it never takes one of the
;     list's PREVIOUS_SHOP_ORDER_LIST_LIMIT places and a customer with many still gets their newest
;     shown orders; the cited serialisation and ordering lines of the list did not move;
;   * the detail, the status and the customer's own cancel answer 404 for it, exactly as for an
;     order that does not exist or is someone else's, so the answer says nothing about what is
;     stored and nothing is written -- a cancel of such an order refunds and changes nothing;
;   * the profile's order count, printed beside the link to the list, counts only the orders the
;     list can show; until this decision it counted every stored row.
; A reorder starts from the list or the detail (every reorder conversion named above reads one of
; them), so it cannot reach such an order either.
; MIXED ORDERS. An order holding a bike line AND a line of a retired kind is this shop's -- a rental
; taken beside the old catalogue before 085 hid it -- and it is shown: its rental is the customer's
; own booking, and hiding it would hide that. Its other lines keep the neutral name above, which
; since this decision is served for that case only (PREVIOUS_SHOP_ORDER_MIXED_IS_SHOWN). The shop
; decides first: an order naming the previous shop is not shown whatever it holds.
; NOT CHANGED. No row is written or deleted and no migration is added. The admin reads -- the list,
; one order, the status change and the payment code -- read every row whole: they are the archive.
; A message the bot sends to a customer when an admin changes such an order's status is the admin's
; act, not a customer read, and it is unchanged. What production stores was not read.
pub const PREVIOUS_SHOP_ORDER_DECIDED_AT : str = "2026-09-26";
pub const PREVIOUS_SHOP_ORDER_DECIDED_BY : str = "owner, read by the operator";
pub const PREVIOUS_SHOP_ORDER_READ_AS : str = "A customer must not see the previous shop's orders at all.";
