; Whether a customer is shown a stored order: never one naming the previous shop, and otherwise
; only one holding a bike line.
pub fn order_presentation_order_is_shown(names_the_previous_shop: bool, holds_a_bike_line: bool) bool {
  if (names_the_previous_shop) {
    return false;
  }
  return holds_a_bike_line;
}

; --- The name of a bike line (2026-09-26) -------------------------------------------------------
; FIXED 2026-09-26. A review of 405f30e found that every bike line printed "Unknown" on both owned
; screens and on the profile's recent orders. Each screen named a line from the old catalogue's
; four name keys only, and a bike line fills none of them: the customer read even serves them empty
; on a bike line (customer_order_line, RETIRED_LINE_RULE). So a bike line fell through to the
; screens' last resort, the keyless English string at two of KEYLESS_CUSTOMER_STRING_SITES. The
; wire does name the bike: a bike line carries its bike as an object with the family key, always,
; and with the brand and model stored when the line was written, sometimes (BikeLine in
; src/db/orders.rs; BIKE_LINE_NAME_FIELDS, in the order they are tried). The rule is that struct's
; own documented fallback and nothing more: the stored name when it has text, else the family key.
; Nothing is looked up -- the order screens hold no catalogue -- and nothing is invented: a line
; that carries neither prints what it printed before. The staff message names a bike line the same
; way (admin_item_line in src/api/orders.rs), so the customer and the shop read one name for one
; line. Each screen's name function tries the bike first and the old keys after it, and the keyless
